8.text:
The essay is *El escritor argentino y la tradición* – ‘The Argentine Writer and the Tradition’, 1932, *Discusión* / *Otras inquisiciones* / *Other Inquisitions*, the patriotic-illusion paragraph where Borges writes *las ilusiones del patriotismo no tienen fin / the illusions of patriotism have no end*.

In the Spanish text the three stock national self-encomia that Borges strings together to illustrate the point are the classic Greek, English and German patriotic commonplaces that he habitually quotes in that paragraph.

* Greek – the Athenian patriotic topos, the Periclean Funeral Oration as transmitted in Thucydides, the Periclean/Herodotean voice of Hellenic superiority that is rendered in different editions as Pericles / Herodotus / Pindar;
* Englishman – the patriotic encomium that Borges habitually adduces for England in the canonical form as Samuel Johnson;
* German – the linguistic-nationalist encomium that Borges adduces for Germany in the Spanish text as Johann Gottfried Herder, the figure most often rendered in English editions as Heinrich Heine and in some renderings as Johann Gottlieb Fichte.

The stable English core of the triad is therefore Pericles, Samuel Johnson, Johann Gottfried Herder.

